on april 26 apple made a change to its  ios software and anyone using an iphone  began to see this the app tracking  transparency prompt apple began  requiring apps to give users a choice  ask them not to track you or allow them  to track you across other apps and  websites  now  months later that one software tweak is  having a huge impact on e-commerce and  while the change gave apple users more  control over their data it may mean that  you'll be paying more for stuff whether  you use apple products or not here's why  first here's a number for you 84 percent  even though apps have a chance to  explain why they want to track your  activity  users are asking apps not to track them  about 84 of the time now that they have  the option to opt out according to app  analytics provider flurry and that has  jolted big tech platforms like facebook  and snapchat and bumble companies that  make money by serving you targeted ads  in recent earnings calls tech executives  have blamed apple for a slowdown in  revenue growth our advertising business  was disrupted by changes to ios ad  tracking overall if it wasn't for  apple's ios 14 changes we would have  seen positive quarter over quarter  revenue growth in a statement apple said  a user's data belongs to them and they  should get to decide whether to share  their data and with whom  [Music]  okay so multi-billion dollar companies  are complaining about revenue why does  any of this matter for you  we'll get to how the change is impacting  your wallet in a minute but first you  have to understand how it's impacting  millions of small businesses that rely  on social media advertising to reach  customers  the biggest impact is because of how  sweeping the change was and how suddenly  it took place as a principal at true  ventures fiona o'donnell mccarthy  invests in early stage startups and  helps get them off the ground she says a  lot of the ceos she's in touch with have  seen their digital marketing costs soar  since they can't target customers as  easily so what's happening across the  board is that advertising is less  precise because customers are opting out  of sharing data across apps it's more  difficult to have a complete picture of  someone's behavior or preferences how  much more are these businesses these  advertisers paying to reach customers  we're seeing companies spend 30 to 100  percent more in order to acquire a  customer online not only are social  media ads less effective right now but  business owners like the founder of  consumer warranty startup upsy say  they've also gotten a lot more expensive  we've seen it jump on facebook of 135  percent just from a cpm standpoint  so it has gotten insanely expensive to  advertise on places like facebook the  owner of belmonelli jewelry says her ad  prices are eight to ten times more  expensive even though the ads themselves  aren't as effective because she can't  easily target customers who might want  her product  my jewelry ad might now show up  in front of  you know some in some 50 year old man  speed and 50 year old men don't buy my  things  so so that's been the big difference  what's more the privacy change has made  it harder for companies to know if their  ads are even working since it's become  harder for social media companies to  track what you buy after you see an ad  so what does all of this mean for you  the consumer well if you're an iphone  user of course you have more control  over your data but even if you're not  this all still applies to you  first you might start paying more for  products  some businesses say they're raising  prices to help offset higher marketing  costs sports nutrition company ath  sports told me it had to raise prices  five to ten percent and boost the price  of standard shipping from five dollars  to eight dollars  and clothing company sozi says that in  addition to supply chain constraints  marketing costs are making it more  urgent for them to do things like reduce  their welcome offer reduce rewards  points and charge more for return  shipping secondly mobile ad executives  say that new apps that would have been  free may start charging you since  they're looking for alternative ways to  make money and third you may notice that  the ads you're used to seeing in your  social media feeds and in real life look  a little different on your devices if  you opt out of tracking you may start  seeing ads that seem out of left field  and in real life you'll notice tech  savvy brands appearing in totally  unexpected old-school places think  billboards and catalogs and even radio  ads  [Music]  we've all felt pressured into buying a  store's overpriced electronics warranty  you're going to hear more and more  digitally native brands on the radio  you're going to see more companies start  podcasts and blogs you're going to see  more companies focus on the inherent  network effects that are part of their  uh physical or digital product  and that's going to be required in order  to scale  meanwhile those big tech companies say  they're currently working on developing  tools that will let advertisers serve  you personalized ads without getting  your personal information  but what those tools look like and how  much those ads cost remains to be seen a  lot of the business owners i talked to  for this story say they appreciate  privacy concerns and understand the need  to strike a balance data can help  companies find consumers but it can also  help consumers find products that we  love  but how much our data is worth and how  much we're willing to share is a  negotiating process that has only just  begun now i'm curious where you stand on  the privacy issue are you willing to  allow apps to track your activity online  or do you prefer to opt out let me know  why by leaving me a comment and if you  want to know more about how tracking  works check out the links below for  videos from my colleague wsj tech  columnist joanna stern and last but not  least if you 